  with star wars with a new hope george  lucas wanted to create  awe-inspiring space chases and battles  and audiences could be right in the  action thanks in part to a man named  john dykstra and his new camera called  the dykstra flats  the dijkstraflex was programmed by a  computer to move around the miniatures  in such a way to create the illusion of  speeding through space like this  imposing angle of the underbelly of a  star destroyer  or this shot during the death star  dogfight that appears to be from a  pilot's point of view  the camera's precise programming also  made shots easily repeatable for  seamless compositing  it's why multiple models could be shot  separately and then combined during the  dog fight  it's also why the death star really  looks like it's floating up in space  when in reality this close-up was shot  in a parking lot  motion control cameras can be seen again  and again in space movies  they were used in the star wars sequels  throughout the 1980s and  all the way through 2014 for the  practical models of interstellar  but all of these movies were about  fictional space travel  things needed to be a lot more realistic  with apollo 13.  houston we have a problem oh boy what's  going on here flight let me get back to  you  so director ron howard put the cast in  actual zero g for wider shots  it was the first movie ever shot on  nasa's kc-135  where real astronauts trained for space  conditions at the time  the plane flies up 35 000 feet in the  air before  plunging back to earth this renders all  passengers weightless for 25 seconds  no wires needed the crew only had 25  seconds per ride to shoot so the  filmmakers broke down each scene into  small chunks  and then later used wires on set for  close-ups  in total the cast and crew took 612  flights  adding up to nearly four hours of  weightlessness  shooting on the vomit comet looks  authentic but the limited zero g windows  make it a logistical nightmare  and what do you do for shots that need  to be much longer  gravity contained harrowing continuous  takes in space that lasted as long as  15 minutes making it difficult to both  move  and light the actors properly we went to  incredible lengths on  on the movie gravity to portray  microgravity or zero gravity  first the crew relied on pre-visualized  animations that showed what the movie  would look like before  shooting took place studying hours of  reference footage to create the digital  characters also helped them  imitate weightlessness more accurately  people do not move  slowly as though they're under water in  space that's a common  misconception if you watch those  astronauts they move really quickly  because there's no air resistance  to tackle the sequence where a debris  storm hits the characters  director alfonso cuaron and  cinematographer emmanuel lubezki  invented a new technology called a light  box  we were going to run for 15 minutes  without a hard cut  we had to be able to plan the lighting  and have perfect continuity because  everything was going to need to sit  together  the light box built on existing  technology sandra bullock and george  clooney were strapped into tilt rigs  while their space journey was captured  by motion control cameras  all of that was inside a box covered  with led panels that could for example  show  real images of earth from space as the  backdrop  because it was all programmed they could  change up the lighting faster than any  normal studio light  which came in handy for the scene where  they're hit by space debris  movies like first man built on the light  box and went even further with  led screens director damien chazelle  used a 35 foot tall 65 foot wide  led screen to provide dynamic 180 degree  views of space in  real time that meant the gemini space  capsule leaving earth could be captured  in camera  and because this life-size model was  motorized the camera didn't need to fake  any movements  you could even get this convincing lunar  